    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "interesting. so when you say they were 'viewed as legally flawed', is that just a general consensus among legal scholars, or is there a specific reason their interpretation of the 14th Amendment was dismissed as incorrect?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The dismissal wasn't just general consensus; the majority focused on the \"penumbras\" argument used by dissenters, which they argued stretched the framers' intent beyond constitutional text. Specifically, the majority asserted that the Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process Clause does not implicitly create rights not explicitly mentioned or deeply rooted in historical practice. Therefore, the dissent's broad view of substantive due process was seen as inventing a right rather than interpreting an established one.",
      "source": "target",
